  "content": {
    "id": "p7-p5-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "Show that $\\log_{xy}(a)=\\dfrac{\\log_x(a)}{1+\\log_x(y)}$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\dfrac{\\log_x(a)}{1+\\log_x(y)}$\n\nChange to base $x$, then expand $\\log_x(xy)$.",
      "worked": "For the given logarithms, $a,x,y>0$, $x\\ne1$ and $xy\\ne1$. Use change of base and the product law.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\log_{xy}(a)&=\\frac{\\log_x(a)}{\\log_x(xy)}\\\\\\log_{xy}(a)&=\\frac{\\log_x(a)}{\\log_x(x)+\\log_x(y)}\\\\\\log_{xy}(a)&=\\frac{\\log_x(a)}{1+\\log_x(y)}.\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 24
  },
